<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuxtla Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sierra de los Tuxtlas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuxtla Mountains, or Sierra de los Tuxtlas, are a volcanic range on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> coast in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Their basalt was quarried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmecs</a> and hauled to San Lorenzo and other centres to carve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a>, table-thrones and other monuments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a> grew on their western edge and remained a regional centre long after the wider Olmec decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains.jpg" alt="Tuxtla Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dongringo at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_martin1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is a land of great rivers, such as the Coatzacoalcos and the Papaloapan, of swamps, forest and seasonal floods, overlooked by the volcanic <b>Tuxtla Mountains</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Basalt was hauled from the <b>Tuxtla Mountains</b> some 60 kilometres away, showing great command of labour.</blockquote><a href="/state/san-lorenzo">San Lorenzo</a></li><li><blockquote>Tres Zapotes grew on the western edge of the <b>Tuxtla Mountains</b> in southern Veracruz around 900 BCE, during the Olmec period.</blockquote><a href="/state/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a></li></ul></section>
